","When \( \text{H}_2\text{S} \) gas is added to a solution containing \( \text{Mn}^{2+}, \text{Ni}^{2+}, \text{Cu}^{2+}, \) and \( \text{Hg}^{2+} \) ions in an acidified aqueous solution, the insoluble sulfides precipitate. The solubility of metal sulfides decreases with the charge density of the metal ion. Here's the order of solubility:

1. **\( \text{CuS} \):** \( \text{Cu}^{2+} \) is less soluble than \( \text{Ni}^{2+} \), \( \text{Hg}^{2+} \), or \( \text{Mn}^{2+} \). \( \text{CuS} \) is insoluble and precipitates.
2. **\( \text{HgS} \):** \( \text{Hg}^{2+} \) is less soluble than \( \text{Ni}^{2+} \) or \( \text{Mn}^{2+} \). \( \text{HgS} \) is insoluble and precipitates.
3. **\( \text{MnS} \):** \( \text{Mn}^{2+} \) is less soluble than \( \text{Ni}^{2+} \). \( \text{MnS} \) is insoluble and precipitates.
4. **\( \text{NiS} \):** \( \text{Ni}^{2+} \) is more soluble than \( \text{Mn}^{2+}, \text{Hg}^{2+}, \) and \( \text{Cu}^{2+} \). Thus, \( \text{NiS} \) remains soluble and does not precipitate.

