</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
The decay length \(l\) of an evanescent wave is given by:
\[
l = \frac{\lambda}{2\pi \left| n_{\text{core}} - n_{\text{cladding}} \right|}
\]
where \(n_{\text{core}}\) and \(n_{\text{cladding}}\right\}\) are the refractive indices of the core and cladding, respectively, and \(\lambda\) is the wavelength.

The given condition is \(n_{\text{eff}} - n_f \sim 0.3\), where \(n_{\text{eff}}\) is the effective refractive index and \(n_f\) is the refractive index of the surrounding medium. Here, the cladding is Ga\(_{0.2}\)Al_{0.8}As, and the surrounding medium is assumed to be free space. Thus, \(n_f = 1\).

The effective index \(n_{\text{eff}}\) is the refractive index of the guide, which is the Ga\(_{0.2}\)Al_{0.8}As cladding in this case. Thus, \(n_{\text{eff}} = n_{\text{cladding}}\).

The given condition \(n_{\text{eff}} - n_f \approx 0.3\) implies \(n_{\text{eff}} - 1 \approx 0.3\), so \(n_{\text{eff}} \approx 1.3\).

The core is GaAs, and the cladding is Ga\(_{0.2}\)Al_{0.2}\)As. The refractive indices of GaAs and AlAs are \(n_{\text{GaAs}} \approx 3.36\) and \(n_{\text{AlAs}} \approx 2.98\), respectively. The refractive index of Ga\(_{0.2}\)Al_{0.8}As is given by:
\[
n_{\text{Ga}_x\text{Al}_{1-x}\text{As}} \approx x n_{\text{GaAs}} + (1-x) n_{\text{AlAs}}
\]
Substituting \(x = 0.2\):
\[
n_{\text{Ga}_{0.2}\text{Al}_{0.8}\text{As}} \approx 0.2 \times 3.36 + 0.8 \times 2.98 = 0.672 + 2.384 = 3.056 \approx 3.06
\]
Thus, \(n_{\text{cladding}} \approx 3.06\).

The refractive index of the core is \(n_{\text{core}} = 3.36\).

The decay length is:
\[
l = \frac{\lambda}{2\pi \left| n_{\text{core}} - n_{\text{cladding}} \right|} = \frac{1 \times 10^{-6}}{2\pi |3.36 - 3.06|} = \frac{10^{-6}}{2\pi \times 0.3} \approx \frac{10^{-6}}{1.885} \approx 0.531 \times 10^{-6} \text{ m} = 531 \text{ nm}
\]

Thus, the decay length is \(\boxed{531 \text{ nm}}\).
