</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
To solve this problem, we first need to find the original and new closed-loop voltages. The voltage divider rule is used to find the voltage at the inverting terminal of the operator amplifier. The voltage divider formula is:

\[
V_f = \frac{A_v}{1 + A_v m_v}
\]

Given:
- Initial voltage gain: \( A_v = 100 \)
- Fraction of feedback: \( m_v = 0.1 \)

Original voltage:
\[
A_{vf,original} = \frac{100}{1 + 100 \times 0.1} = \frac{100}{1 + 10} = \frac{100}{11} \approx 9.09 \, \text{V}
\]

The voltage drop is 6 dB, which means the new voltage gain is:
\[
A_{v,new} = 100 \times 10^{-6/10} = 100 \times 10^{-0.6} \approx 100 \times 0.501 = 50.1 \, \text{V}
\]

New voltage:
\[
A_{vf,new} = \frac{50.1}{1 + 50.1 \times 0.1} = \frac{50.1}{1 + 5.01} = \frac{50.1}{6.01} \approx 8.33 \, \text{V}
\]

Percentage change in the voltage:
\[
\text{Percentage change} = \left( \frac{A_{vf,new} - A_{vf,original}}{A_{vf,original}} \right) \times 100 = \left( \frac{8.33 - 9.09}{9.09} \right) \times 100 = \left( \frac{-0.76}{9.09} \right) \times 100 \approx -8.36\%
\]

Thus, the percentage change in the closed-loop voltage is approximately \(\boxed{-8.36\%}\).
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

