What is the maximum percentage change in the wavelength of a 2.00 Å photon scattered by an electron?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","The maximum change in wavelength for Compton scattering occurs when the electron is scattered back towards the incident photon. In this case, the energy and momentum of the photon are conserved, as is the energy and momentum of the electron. The Compton scattering formula relates the change in wavelength (\(\Delta\lambda\)) to the wavelength of the incident photon (\(\lambda\)) and the Compton wavelength (\(\lambda_c\)) of the electron:

\[
\Delta\lambda = \lambda_c \left( \frac{1}{1 - \cos \theta} \right)
\]

where \(\theta\) is the scattering angle. The maximum change in wavelength occurs when \(\theta = 0^\circ\), which corresponds to the photon being scattered back (i.e., \(\theta = 180^\circ\)). Therefore, \(\cos \theta = -1\), and:

\[
\Delta\lambda_{\text{max}} = \lambda_c \left( \frac{1}{1 - (-1)} \right) = \lambda_c \left( \frac{1}{2} \right)
\]

The Compton wavelength \(\lambda_c\) is given by:

\[
\lambda_c = \frac{h}{m_e c}
\]

where \(h\) is Planck's constant, \(m_e\) is the mass of the electron, and \(c\) is the speed of light. Substituting the values:

\[
\lambda_c = \frac{6.626 \times 10^{-34} \, \text{J s}}{(9.109 \times 10^{-31} \, \text{kg})(3.00 \times 10^8 \, \text{m/s})} \approx 2.427 \times 10^{-10} \, \text{m} = 0.2427 \, \text{Å}
\]

Now, we can find the maximum change in wavelength:

\[
\Delta\lambda_{\text{max}} = 0.2427 \, \text{Å} \times \frac{1}{2} = 0.12135 \, \text{Å}
\]

The initial wavelength is \(\lambda = 2.00 \, \text{Å}\). The percentage change in wavelength is:

\[
\text{Percentage Change} = \left( \frac{\Delta\lambda}{\lambda} \right) \times 100 = \left( \frac{0.12135}{2.00} \right) \times 100 \approx 6.0675\%
\]

Thus, the maximum percentage change in the wavelength of the photon is \(\boxed{6.07\%}\).",fluency,"# Task Description
