What is the net chemical reaction that occurs during the discharging of a lead storage battery?
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
The net chemical reaction that occurs during the discharging of a lead-acid (lead-saddle) series cell is:  
\[
\text{Pb} + \text{H}_2\text{SO}_4 \rightarrow \text{PbSO}_4 + 2\text{H}^+
\]  
This reaction occurs at the negative electrode (anode).  

Simultaneous with this, at the positive electrode (cathode), the reaction is:  
\[
\text{PbO}_2 + \text{H}_2\text{SO}_4 + 2\text{H}^+ \rightarrow \text{PbSO}_4 + 2\text{H}_2\text{O}
\]  

The overall, or net, reaction is the sum of these two half-reactions:  
\[
\text{Pb} + \text{PbO}_2 + 2\text{H}_2\text{SO}_4 \rightarrow 2\text{PbSO}_4 + 2\text{H}_2\text{O}
\]  

Thus, the net reaction is:  
\[
\boxed{\text{Pb} + \text{PbO}_2 + 2\text{H}_2\text{SO}_4 \rightarrow 2\text{PbSO}_4 + 2\text{H}_2\text{O}}
\]  
During discharge, lead (Pb) and lead dioxide (PbO₂) are converted to lead sulfate (PbSO₄), and sulfate ions (SO₄²⁻) from the electrolyte are consumed, forming lead sulfate and water.
