\[
\sum F = \Delta m_0 v = \dot{m} (v_2 - v_1)
\]

where $\Delta m_0$ is the mass of the fluid, $v_1$ and $v_2$ are the flow velocities at the inlet and outlet, and $\dot{m}$ is the mass flow rate.

The flowmeter consists of a conical tube with a central conduit. Air flows uniformly around the conduit. The flow velocity at the top of the conduit is equal to the inlet velocity. The net force on the conduit is the sum of the gravitational force (weight) and the pressure force due to the flow. The weight acts downward, and the pressure force is the difference between the pressure at the top and bottom of the conduit.

The flow flow rate is $\dot{m} = 0.3 \, \text{kg/s}$. The inlet conditions are $T = 20^\circ \text{C}$ and $P = 1 \, \text{atm}$. The density of air at these conditions is:
\[
\rho = \rho_0 \left( \frac{T}{T_0} \right) \left( \frac{P}{P_0} \right)^{\frac{1}{T}}
\]
where $\rho_0 = 1.225 \, \text{kg/m}^3$ (at 0°C), $T_0 = 273.15 \, \text{K}$, $P_0 = 101325 \, \text{Pa}$, and $T = 293.15 \, \text{K}$.
\[
\rho = 1.225 \times \frac{293.15}{273.15} \times \left( \frac{101325}{101325} \right)^{1/1} = 1.225 \times 1.071 \, \text{kg/m}^3 = 1.31 \, \text{kg/m}^3
