","To verify the unitarity condition for the S-matrix, we start with the definition of the S-matrix:  
\[
S_{ij} = \frac{1}{N_o} \sum_k \langle i | \mathcal{T} | k \rangle \langle k | \mathcal{T}^{-1} | j \rangle
\]  
Here, \(i, j, k\) are the eigenstates of the Hermitian operator \(H\), \(N_o\) is a normalization constant, and \(\mathcal{T}\) is the operator for time distribution. The uncertainty condition ensures that the time-evolution operator is independent of the choice of the eigenstate. The eigenvalues of \(H\) are \(E_i, E_j, E_k\). The Dirac integral is:  
\[
\langle i | \mathcal{T} | k \rangle = \int \frac{dE}{2\pi} e^{i E (t - t') \hat{E}} \langle i | \hat{a}(t') | k \rangle
\]  
The Dirac integral yields:  
\[
\langle i | \mathcal{T} | k \rangle = \frac{1}{E_i - E_k} e^{i (E_i - E_k) t}
\]  
Thus, the matrix element of the S-matrix is:  
\[
S_{ij} = \frac{1}{N_o} \sum_k \frac{1}{E_k - E_i} e^{i (E_k - E_i) t} \frac{1}{E_k - E_j} e^{i (E_j - E_k) t}
\]  
Simplifying the exponential term:  
\[
S_{ij} = \frac{1}{N_o} \sum_k \frac{1}{(E_k - E_i)(E_k - E_j)} e^{i (E_j - E_i) t}
\]  
The normalization condition is:  
\[
N_o = \frac{1}{2\pi}
\]  
Thus, the matrix element becomes:  
\[
S_{ij} = \frac{1}{2\pi i} \sum_k \frac{1}{(E_k - E_i)(E_k - E_j)} e^{i (E_j - E_i) t}
\]  
The conjugate of \(S_{ij}\) is:  
\[
S_{ij}^* = \frac{1}{2\pi i} \sum_k \frac{1}{(E_k - E_i)(E_k - E_j)} e^{-i (E_j - E_i) t}
\]  
The unitarity condition is:  
\[
\sum_l S_{im}^* S_{il} = \delta_{mj}
\]  
Substituting the expression for \(S_{ij}\):  
\[
\sum_l \left( \frac{1}{2\pi i} \sum_k \frac{1}{(E_k - E_i)(E_k - E_l)} e^{-i (E_l - E_i) t} \right) \left( \frac{1}{2\pi i} \sum_l \frac{1}{(E_l - E_i)(E_l - E_j)} e^{i (E_j - E_i) t} \",ground_truth_similarity,"# Task Description
